<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <h2>What is the difference between neglect and abuse in a nursing home?</h2>
  <p>Neglect is the failure to provide basic care: food, fluids, hygiene, medical attention, and supervision. Abuse is intentional harmful conduct: physical, emotional, sexual, or financial. Both are actionable; abuse typically produces higher damages because courts add punitive damages for intentional misconduct.</p>
  <h2>What is a stage 3 or stage 4 pressure sore?</h2>
  <p>Pressure sores (decubitus ulcers) are staged by severity. Stage 3: full-thickness skin loss extending to subcutaneous tissue. Stage 4: full-thickness tissue loss with exposed bone, tendon, or muscle. Stage 3 and 4 sores in nursing home residents are presumptively caused by neglect (inadequate repositioning and hygiene). They are painful, dangerous, and often preventable.</p>
  <h2>Who can file a nursing home neglect claim?</h2>
  <p>The resident directly. If the resident lacks capacity, a guardian, conservator, or healthcare proxy can file on their behalf. If the resident has died, the estate or surviving family members can bring a wrongful death claim. Adult children and spouses are typical plaintiffs in wrongful death and survival action claims.</p>
  <h2>What is the statute of limitations?</h2>
  <p>Varies by state and claim type: 2–3 years for negligence/malpractice; sometimes longer under elder abuse statutes. If the victim died, the wrongful death statute of limitations applies (typically 2 years from death). Consult an elder law attorney promptly — evidence is time-sensitive.</p>
  <h2>What should I do if I suspect nursing home abuse?</h2>
  <p>Report to the state long-term care ombudsman and Adult Protective Services immediately. Request an investigation. Document all injuries with photographs. Transfer the resident if the situation is unsafe. Preserve medical records by formally requesting copies before they can be altered.</p>
</BaseLayout>
